FaceIdentify schema migrations (PERSISTENCE_IMPLEMENTATION.md §27).

Alembic is the only way the production schema is created or changed: never call
`Base.metadata.create_all()` outside a test that is comparing against it.

The library database's path comes from the FACEIDENTIFY_DATABASE_PATH environment variable
(no Storage Manager exists yet to resolve it). Run from the repository root:

    $env:FACEIDENTIFY_DATABASE_PATH = "C:\path\to\library.db"
    uv run alembic upgrade head                 # create/upgrade the schema
    uv run alembic upgrade head --sql           # print the SQL to review (it has no BEGIN/COMMIT:
                                                # do not pipe it into a database)
    uv run alembic downgrade base               # drop every table: DEVELOPMENT ONLY
    uv run alembic check                        # fail if the models drifted from the migrations

Migrations run with foreign key enforcement off (SQLite cannot recreate a referenced table
otherwise) and check `PRAGMA foreign_key_check` before each revision commits; each revision is its
own transaction, so one that fails leaves the database at the revision before it. Triggers belong
to their table: recreating `processing_configuration_snapshots` would drop its two immutability
triggers (revision 0003), so a revision that does must recreate them (the triggers' text is
`SNAPSHOT_*_TRIGGER` in `backend/app/processing/models.py`). The triggers do not stop `INSERT OR
REPLACE` on a snapshot no run references (SQLite does not fire delete triggers for it): issue 55. The `--sql`
output is for review only: it has neither the foreign key pragma nor the check, so do not replay it.

`downgrade base` destroys the library. Foreign keys sometimes stop it on a database that has rows
(atomically), but whether they do is data-dependent, so never rely on that. Production only
upgrades.

Changing a model means adding a reviewed revision:

    uv run alembic revision --autogenerate --rev-id 0002 -m "short description"

Then read the generated file: autogenerate does not detect CHECK-constraint changes, and it does
not know about data. SQLite can only change most constraints by recreating the table, so use
`op.batch_alter_table` (batch mode is already on in env.py), passing the table as a frozen
definition (`copy_from`, see revision 0002) so `upgrade --sql` keeps working. tests/integration/test_migrations.py
fails if the models and the migrations disagree.
